INSERT INTO `ai_messages` (`id`, `conversation_id`, `role`, `content`, `tool_name`, `tool_call_id`, `created_at`) VALUES (32, 'f5166306-9ff8-4542-b5f0-aac21f1740e6', 'user', 'give the java code', NULL, NULL, '2026-09-22 06:19:23');
INSERT INTO `ai_messages` (`id`, `conversation_id`, `role`, `content`, `tool_name`, `tool_call_id`, `created_at`) VALUES (33, 'f5166306-9ff8-4542-b5f0-aac21f1740e6', 'assistant', 'Here’s a simple, self‑contained Java example that shows how you might model an **Employee** object, keep a list of employees, and print the list to the console.  \nYou can copy‑paste it into a file called `EmployeeDemo.java` and run it with any Java 8+ JDK.\n\n```java\nimport java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.List;\n\n/**\n * A very basic Employee class with a few common fields.\n */\nclass Employee {\n    private final int id;\n    private final String name;\n    private final String department;\n\n    public Employee(int id, String name, String department) {\n        this.id = id;\n        this.name = name;\n        this.department = department;\n    }\n\n    // Getters (you could also add setters if you need mutability)\n    public int getId() { return id; }\n    public String getName() { return name; }\n    public String getDepartment() { return department; }\n\n    @Override\n    public String toString() {\n        return String.format(\"Employee{id=%d, name=\'%s\', department=\'%s\'}\",\n                id, name, department);\n    }\n}\n\n/**\n * Demo class that creates a few employees, stores them in a List,\n * and prints the list.\n */\npublic class EmployeeDemo {\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        // 1️⃣ Create a mutable list to hold employees\n        List<Employee> employeeList = new ArrayList<>();\n\n        // 2️⃣ Add some sample employees\n        employeeList.add(new Employee(101, \"Alice Johnson\", \"Finance\"));\n        employeeList.add(new Employee(102, \"Bob Smith\",    \"Engineering\"));\n        employeeList.add(new Employee(103, \"Carol Lee\",    \"Human Resources\"));\n\n        // 3️⃣ Print all employees\n        System.out.println(\"Current employee roster:\");\n        for (Employee emp : employeeList) {\n            System.out.println(emp);\n        }\n\n        // Optional: Find an employee by ID\n        int searchId = 102;\n        Employee found = findById(employeeList, searchId);\n        if (found != null) {\n            System.out.println(\"\\nFound employee with ID \" + searchId + \": \" + found);\n        } else {\n            System.out.println(\"\\nNo employee found with ID \" + searchId);\n        }\n    }\n\n    /**\n     * Helper method that searches the list for an employee with the given ID.\n     *\n     * @param list the employee list\n     * @param id   the ID to look for\n     * @return the matching Employee, or null if none found\n     */\n    private static Employee findById(List<Employee> list, int id) {\n        for (Employee emp : list) {\n            if (emp.getId() == id) {\n                return emp;\n            }\n        }\n        return null;\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### What the code does\n| Step | Description |\n|------|-------------|\n| **Employee class** | Holds `id`, `name`, and `department`. It’s immutable (fields are `final`) and overrides `toString()` for readable output. |\n| **List creation** | Uses `ArrayList<Employee>` – a dynamic array that lets you add/remove employees at runtime. |\n| **Adding employees** | Demonstrates how to instantiate `Employee` objects and add them to the list. |\n| **Printing** | Loops through the list and prints each employee via the overridden `toString()`. |\n| **Search helper** | Shows a simple linear search (`findById`) that returns an employee if the ID matches. |\n\n### How to run\n1. Save the code as `EmployeeDemo.java`.\n2. Open a terminal/command prompt and compile:  \n   ```bash\n   javac EmployeeDemo.java\n   ```\n3. Run the compiled class:  \n   ```bash\n   java EmployeeDemo\n   ```\n\nYou’ll see output similar to:\n\n```\nCurrent employee roster:\nEmployee{id=101, name=\'Alice Johnson\', department=\'Finance\'}\nEmployee{id=102, name=\'Bob Smith\', department=\'Engineering\'}\nEmployee{id=103, name=\'Carol Lee\', department=\'Human Resources\'}\n\nFound employee with ID 102: Employee{id=102, name=\'Bob Smith\', department=\'Engineering\'}\n```\n\nFeel free to extend this skeleton:\n- Add more fields (e.g., email, hire date).  \n- Implement CRUD operations (create, read, update, delete).  \n- Persist data to a file or database.  \n- Use streams (`employeeList.stream().filter(...).collect(...)`) for more advanced queries.  \n\nHappy coding! 🚀', NULL, NULL, '2026-09-22 06:19:27');
