INSERT INTO `ai_messages` (`id`, `conversation_id`, `role`, `content`, `tool_name`, `tool_call_id`, `created_at`) VALUES (35, 'f5166306-9ff8-4542-b5f0-aac21f1740e6', 'assistant', '## Java – A High‑Level Overview\n\nJava is a **general‑purpose, object‑oriented programming language** that was created by Sun Microsystems (now owned by Oracle) in the mid‑1990s. Its most famous slogan, **“Write once, run anywhere,”** captures the core design goal: a program written in Java should run on any device that has a Java Virtual Machine (JVM), without needing to be re‑compiled for each platform.\n\nBelow is a concise but comprehensive rundown of the language’s key concepts, architecture, and everyday usage.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Java Platform Stack\n| Layer | What it is | Main responsibilities |\n|-------|------------|------------------------|\n| **JDK (Java Development Kit)** | Tools for developers (compiler `javac`, debugger, JAR tool, etc.) | Write, compile, test, package Java code |\n| **JRE (Java Runtime Environment)** | Runtime only (JVM + core libraries) | Execute compiled bytecode |\n| **JVM (Java Virtual Machine)** | Abstract machine that loads, verifies, and executes **bytecode** | Provides platform independence, garbage collection, security sandbox |\n| **Standard Library (Java SE API)** | Thousands of ready‑made classes (`java.util`, `java.io`, `java.net`, `java.time`, …) | Common data structures, I/O, networking, concurrency, etc. |\n\nWhen you run `java MyApp`, the JVM loads the compiled `.class` files (bytecode), links them with the standard library, and interprets/just‑in‑time (JIT) compiles them to native machine code.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Core Language Concepts\n\n| Concept | Description | Example |\n|---------|--------------|---------|\n| **Classes & Objects** | Blueprint (`class`) → instances (`object`). Supports encapsulation, inheritance, polymorphism. | `class Person { private String name; public void speak() { … } }` |\n| **Methods** | Functions bound to a class. Can be `static` (class‑level) or instance methods. | `public static int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }` |\n| **Fields** | Variables inside a class. Can be instance (`int age;`) or static (`static final double PI = 3.14;`). |\n| **Interfaces** | Pure abstract contracts (methods without bodies) that classes can implement. | `interface Drawable { void draw(Graphics g); }` |\n| **Enums** | Type‑safe enumerations with optional fields/methods. | `enum Day { MON, TUE, WED, THU, FRI, SAT, SUN }` |\n| **Generics** | Compile‑time type safety for collections and other generic classes. | `List<String> names = new ArrayList<>();` |\n| **Annotations** | Metadata added to code, processed at compile‑time or runtime. | `@Override`, `@Deprecated`, custom `@Entity` (JPA). |\n| **Exception Handling** | Checked (`IOException`) and unchecked (`NullPointerException`) exceptions, using `try/catch/finally`. | `try { … } catch (IOException e) { … }` |\n| **Packages** | Namespace grouping; also maps to folder structure. | `package com.mycompany.app;` |\n| **Modules (Java 9+)** | Explicit dependencies between groups of packages (`module-info.java`). | `module com.myapp { requires java.sql; exports com.myapp.api; }` |\n\n---\n\n### 3. Syntax Basics (Hello‑World)\n\n```java\n// File: HelloWorld.java\npublic class HelloWorld {               // class declaration (public = visible to JVM)\n    public static void main(String[] args) { // entry point\n        System.out.println(\"Hello, world!\"); // prints to console\n    }\n}\n```\n\n* Compile: `javac HelloWorld.java` → produces `HelloWorld.class`.\n* Run: `java HelloWorld` → JVM loads the bytecode and executes `main`.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Memory Model & Garbage Collection\n\n| Area | What lives here | Typical size |\n|------|-----------------|--------------|\n| **Stack** | Primitive values, object references, method frames | Small, per‑thread |\n| **Heap** | All actual objects (`new`), arrays | Large, shared across threads |\n| **Method Area** (part of heap) | Class metadata, static fields, JIT‑compiled code | Fixed per class loader |\n\nThe **Garbage Collector (GC)** automatically reclaims heap memory that is no longer reachable from any live thread. Java offers several GC algorithms (Serial, Parallel, CMS, G1, ZGC, Shenandoah) that can be tuned via JVM flags.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Concurrency Model\n\n* **Threads** – `java.lang.Thread` or `java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService`.\n* **Synchronization** – `synchronized` blocks/methods, `Lock` objects.\n* **High‑level utilities** – `java.util.concurrent` package: `Future`, `CompletableFuture`, `BlockingQueue`, `ConcurrentHashMap`, `Atomic*` classes.\n* **Memory consistency** – Happens‑before relationships defined by the Java Memory Model (JMM) guarantee visibility across threads.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Common Development Tools\n\n| Tool | Purpose |\n|------|---------|\n| **Maven / Gradle** | Build automation, dependency management, packaging. |\n| **IDE** (IntelliJ IDEA, Eclipse, VS Code) | Code editing, refactoring, debugging, UI designers. |\n| **JUnit / TestNG** | Unit testing frameworks. |\n| **SpotBugs / Checkstyle / PMD** | Static analysis for bugs & style. |\n| **Docker** | Containerize Java apps for reproducible deployment. |\n| **CI/CD** (GitHub Actions, Jenkins, GitLab) | Automated builds, tests, deployments. |\n\n---\n\n### 7. Ecosystem Highlights\n\n| Area | Popular Frameworks/Libraries |\n|------|------------------------------|\n| **Web** | Spring Boot, Jakarta EE (formerly Java EE), Micronaut, Quarkus |\n| **Persistence** | Hibernate (JPA), MyBatis, Spring Data |\n| **UI** | JavaFX (modern desktop UI), Swing (legacy), Vaadin (web UI) |\n| **Reactive** | Project Reactor, RxJava |\n| **Testing** | JUnit 5, Mockito, AssertJ |\n| **Build** | Maven, Gradle |\n| **Cloud/Native** | Spring Cloud, Micronaut, Quarkus, OpenJDK distributions (AdoptOpenJDK, Amazon Corretto) |\n\n---\n\n### 8. Typical Project Structure (Maven example)\n\n```\nmy-app/\n ├─ src/\n │   ├─ main/\n │   │   ├─ java/\n │   │   │   └─ com/example/app/\n │   │   │        ├─ App.java\n │   │   │        └─ service/\n │   │   │              └─ GreetingService.java\n │   │   └─ resources/\n │   │        └─ application.yml\n └─ pom.xml   ← Maven descriptor (dependencies, plugins, Java version)\n```\n\nRunning `mvn clean package` produces a **fat JAR** (`target/my-app-1.0.jar`) that can be launched with `java -jar target/my-app-1.0.jar`.\n\n---\n\n### 9. Frequently Asked “Why Java?” Questions\n\n| Question | Answer |\n|----------|--------|\n| **Is Java fast?** | Modern JVMs use sophisticated JIT compilation, adaptive optimizations, and GC tuning, making Java performance comparable to native C/C++ for many workloads. |\n| **Is Java still relevant?** | Absolutely. Over 9 million developers, massive enterprise codebases, and a vibrant ecosystem (Spring, Android, big‑data tools like Hadoop, Spark). |\n| **What about Android?** | Android uses a **Dalvik/ART** runtime that executes a variant of Java bytecode (DEX). Java syntax and many libraries are shared, though the standard library differs. |\n| **Is Java a “scripting” language?** | No. Java is compiled to bytecode, not interpreted line‑by‑line. However, tools like **JShell** (Java 9+) provide a REPL for rapid experimentation. |\n| **What’s the difference between JDK and JRE?** | JDK = development kit (compiler, tools) + JRE. JRE = runtime only (JVM + core libs). End users need only the JRE (or just the JVM bundled with the application). |\n\n---\n\n### 10. Quick “Hello, Java!” Checklist\n\n1. **Install JDK** (e.g., `jdk-21` from Oracle, OpenJDK, or a vendor distribution).  \n   ```bash\n   java -version\n   javac -version\n   ```\n2. **Write a class** with a `public static void main(String[] args)` method.  \n3. **Compile** with `javac MyClass.java`.  \n4. **Run** with `java MyClass`.  \n\nYou’re now ready to explore anything from simple console utilities to massive micro', NULL, NULL, '2026-09-22 06:19:57');

-- Table structure for appraisals
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS `appraisals`;
CREATE TABLE `appraisals` (
  `id` int NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,
  `employee_id` int NOT NULL,
  `appraisal_cycle` varchar(100) COLLATE utf8mb4_general_ci DEFAULT NULL,
  `rating` decimal(3,2) DEFAULT NULL,
  `feedback` text COLLATE utf8mb4_general_ci,
  `status` varchar(100) COLLATE utf8mb4_general_ci DEFAULT 'In Progress',
  `created_at` timestamp NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,
  `updated_at` timestamp NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP ON UPDATE CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,
  `current_salary` decimal(15,2) DEFAULT NULL,
  `proposed_salary` decimal(15,2) DEFAULT NULL,
  `effective_date` date DEFAULT NULL,
  `appraisal_percentage` decimal(5,2) DEFAULT NULL,
  `remarks` text COLLATE utf8mb4_general_ci,
  `created_by` int DEFAULT NULL,
  `updated_by` int DEFAULT NULL,
  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),
  KEY `appraisals_ibfk_1` (`employee_id`),
  CONSTRAINT `appraisals_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`employee_id`) REFERENCES `employees` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE
) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb4 COLLATE=utf8mb4_general_ci;

